WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.460
[Dr. Emily Zarka]
Imagine a calling to
you from the darkness,

00:00:02.544 --> 00:00:05.422
the voice of someone you
loved who recently died.

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:06.923
You're terrified,

00:00:07.006 --> 00:00:08.925
but you can't ignore
the hypnotic promise

00:00:09.008 --> 00:00:10.593
of seeing them again.

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:14.472
You venture outside to find
the source, a fatal mistake.

00:00:14.556 --> 00:00:16.182
This is the lure of the Nishi.

00:00:16.266 --> 00:00:18.184
Hailing from Bengali
folk tradition,

00:00:18.268 --> 00:00:21.187
these cruel monsters are
also known as Nishi Daak,

00:00:21.271 --> 00:00:23.898
which roughly translates
to call of the darkness

00:00:23.982 --> 00:00:25.525
or the call of the night.

00:00:25.608 --> 00:00:28.403
The lines between
demon spirit and deity

00:00:28.486 --> 00:00:31.781
are often blurred in cultures
of the Indian subcontinent.

00:00:31.865 --> 00:00:33.742
The clear
good-versus-evil dualism

00:00:33.825 --> 00:00:35.702
seen more commonly in
the western hemisphere

00:00:35.785 --> 00:00:37.996
doesn't exist in the same way.

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:40.749
The Nishi falls firmly
into the malevolent end

00:00:40.832 --> 00:00:42.250
of ghostly beings

00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:45.003
and teaches us a lot about
the culture it comes from.

00:00:45.086 --> 00:00:46.629
[rousing music]

00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:50.258
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka,
and this is "Monstrum."

00:00:51.301 --> 00:00:53.720
The Nishi Daak is one of
many bhoots, or ghosts,

00:00:53.803 --> 00:00:55.472
in Bengali tradition.

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:58.892
There are so many types of
ghost stories in Bengal.

00:00:58.975 --> 00:01:01.895
By Bengal, I mean the
country of Bangladesh

00:01:01.978 --> 00:01:05.899
as well as West Bengal
in India, the state.

00:01:05.982 --> 00:01:08.151
We used to all be
one big region.

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:11.362
Of course, after the
partition of India in 1947

00:01:11.446 --> 00:01:13.198
when we achieved
our independence,

00:01:13.281 --> 00:01:15.742
we became two
separate countries.

00:01:15.825 --> 00:01:20.455
But our stories and our
folklore traverse those borders

00:01:20.538 --> 00:01:25.543
of both nationality,
religion, class, and the like.

00:01:26.085 --> 00:01:27.587
That's Dr. Sayantani DasGupta,

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:29.506
assistant professor
at Columbia University

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:31.716
and children's and YA author.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.009
[DasGupta] But the Nishi

00:01:33.092 --> 00:01:36.971
is a slightly different
malevolent entity

00:01:37.055 --> 00:01:40.767
than the rest of
Bengali ghost lore.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:42.560
Tales of these
harmful night spirits

00:01:42.644 --> 00:01:45.146
are prevalent in small
and rural villages.

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:48.525
They pose a deadly threat to
those who wander at night.

00:01:48.608 --> 00:01:51.486
Nishi call out to peaceful
sleepers in a familiar voice,

00:01:51.569 --> 00:01:54.072
usually a deceased loved one.

00:01:54.155 --> 00:01:56.241
One story I've heard
about the Nishi

00:01:56.324 --> 00:02:01.704
is that they are a
kind of disembodied,

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:05.166
ghostly form that can call you,

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.169
mimicking the voice
of a loved one.

00:02:08.253 --> 00:02:10.839
So, for instance, if at night,

00:02:10.922 --> 00:02:14.551
walking by a deserted
bamboo forest,

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:17.428
you were to hear somebody
calling your name,

00:02:17.512 --> 00:02:18.555
somebody very familiar,

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:23.476
for instance, an aunt
or a beloved cousin

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:27.021
calling you maybe even in a
nickname that only they know,

00:02:28.147 --> 00:02:30.900
don't turn around

00:02:30.984 --> 00:02:33.278
because it may be a Nishi

00:02:33.361 --> 00:02:36.990
trying to trick you
out of your spirit.

00:02:37.073 --> 00:02:38.491
While the monster
adopts the voice

00:02:38.575 --> 00:02:40.159
of someone their victim knows,

00:02:40.243 --> 00:02:43.246
these malevolent spirits
aren't necessarily the ghost

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:45.874
of the person's voice
they are adopting.

00:02:45.957 --> 00:02:47.625
The complex nature of Nishi

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:49.752
is reflective of the
spiritual community

00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:52.255
from which these
monsters originate.

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:55.925
The very idea of a ghost
becomes additionally complex

00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:58.052
in cultures where
spiritual practices

00:02:58.136 --> 00:03:00.597
center around the
ideas of reincarnation,

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:03.892
ancestral worship, and
karma, like Hinduism.

00:03:03.975 --> 00:03:06.853
In Hinduism, the subtle
body or second body

00:03:06.936 --> 00:03:08.271
is the part of a person

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:11.190
that is reincarnated into
another physical form.

00:03:11.274 --> 00:03:14.736
The deceased corporeal form
must be completely obliterated

00:03:14.819 --> 00:03:16.613
so the subtle body can move on,

00:03:16.696 --> 00:03:18.907
hence the preference
for cremation.

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:20.617
This also means Bengali ghosts

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.493
don't have gravesites to haunt.

00:03:22.577 --> 00:03:25.830
Bengalis, and particularly
Hindu Bengalis,

00:03:25.914 --> 00:03:29.751
have the notion of
rebirth and reincarnation,

00:03:29.834 --> 00:03:34.380
and so Bengali ghosts are
sometimes perceived of

00:03:34.464 --> 00:03:36.925
as recently deceased people

00:03:37.008 --> 00:03:41.054
who have not been able to enter
the cycle of reincarnation.

00:03:41.137 --> 00:03:43.389
But sometimes Bengali ghosts

00:03:43.473 --> 00:03:46.267
are actually thought
of as creatures,

00:03:46.351 --> 00:03:48.978
monsters of their own kind,

00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:52.941
a kind of different species
in and of themselves.

00:03:53.024 --> 00:03:55.360
Now, Nishi or Nishi Daak

00:03:55.443 --> 00:04:00.406
are not exactly among
this category of spirit

00:04:02.367 --> 00:04:05.036
because they exist in
a far more sinister

00:04:05.119 --> 00:04:07.830
and disembodied form,

00:04:07.914 --> 00:04:11.167
unlike a lot of
other Bengali ghosts.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:14.087
An exclusively malevolent
spirit like a Nishi

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:17.966
can be the result of incomplete
or improper funerary rites.

00:04:18.049 --> 00:04:20.301
The deceased fails to
transition their subtle body

00:04:20.385 --> 00:04:22.428
and thus cannot be reincarnated.

00:04:22.512 --> 00:04:24.639
And since no one
wants more Nishi,

00:04:24.722 --> 00:04:27.433
protecting and executing
cultural burial rights

00:04:27.517 --> 00:04:29.102
is paramount.

00:04:29.185 --> 00:04:31.688
If we see the call of a Nishi
as a way the spiritual body

00:04:31.771 --> 00:04:34.524
continues to link itself
to the physical body,

00:04:34.607 --> 00:04:36.567
the corporeality of the voice

00:04:36.651 --> 00:04:39.153
becomes particularly
interesting.

00:04:39.237 --> 00:04:40.822
What if a Nishi cannot be heard?

00:04:40.905 --> 00:04:42.323
Does it cease to exist?

00:04:42.407 --> 00:04:46.369
Or does it become even
more ambiguous and ominous?

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:47.954
The fact that the Nishi

00:04:48.037 --> 00:04:49.998
can steal or mimic the voice
of a deceased loved one

00:04:50.081 --> 00:04:52.834
seems particularly
threatening in this sense.

00:04:52.917 --> 00:04:56.587
It suggests we cannot fully
trust the dead or our own ears

00:04:56.671 --> 00:04:59.007
in such a way that it
will cause our demise,

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:01.926
which, quite frankly,
is pretty terrifying.

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:04.512
Yet the call of the
Nishi is hard to ignore.

00:05:04.595 --> 00:05:08.391
Only the chosen victim can hear
the Nishi's hypnotic voice.

00:05:08.474 --> 00:05:11.185
Interestingly, these beings
cannot call out to you

00:05:11.269 --> 00:05:12.937
more than two times,

00:05:13.021 --> 00:05:14.355
so superstition dictates

00:05:14.439 --> 00:05:16.983
that you never respond to anyone
calling your name at night

00:05:17.066 --> 00:05:19.861
unless you hear it three times.

00:05:19.944 --> 00:05:22.113
Those who do fall under
the influence of a Nishi

00:05:22.196 --> 00:05:23.740
fall into a trance

00:05:23.823 --> 00:05:27.118
and will follow the distant
voices to a remote area.

00:05:27.201 --> 00:05:29.829
And these nighttime
strolls are dangerous.

00:05:29.912 --> 00:05:32.457
If one is unable to break
the Nishi's influence,

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:35.460
they disappear,
presumably killed.

00:05:35.543 --> 00:05:38.546
While the Nishi can call to
or even grab their victims,

00:05:38.629 --> 00:05:41.799
visual details of the
monster tend to be vague.

00:05:41.883 --> 00:05:43.259
In some accounts,

00:05:43.342 --> 00:05:45.428
the Nishi appears as a
shadowy or indistinct form

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:47.472
resembling the person's
voice they mimic.

00:05:47.555 --> 00:05:50.892
In others, they are an
all-white, misty entity

00:05:50.975 --> 00:05:53.061
with a vaguely human shape.

00:05:53.144 --> 00:05:55.313
The ambiguity highlights
its mutability

00:05:55.396 --> 00:05:58.441
and lets the
imagination run wild.

00:05:58.524 --> 00:06:01.819
In one story translated
by Dr. Suchitra Samanta,

00:06:01.903 --> 00:06:04.739
a Nishi directs its
attention to a little boy.

00:06:04.822 --> 00:06:05.865
The boy shared a close bond

00:06:05.948 --> 00:06:08.326
with his aunt who
helped raise him.

00:06:08.409 --> 00:06:09.619
He would lovingly catch

00:06:09.702 --> 00:06:12.288
his aunt's favorite
freshwater fish to eat.

00:06:12.371 --> 00:06:13.539
When she died suddenly,

00:06:13.623 --> 00:06:15.583
the boy was too
devastated by grief

00:06:15.666 --> 00:06:18.419
to perform his
aunt's last rites.

00:06:18.503 --> 00:06:21.881
He would not eat or speak,
becoming ill himself.

00:06:21.964 --> 00:06:24.801
Exorcists were brought in,
amulets and charms applied,

00:06:24.884 --> 00:06:26.511
but nothing worked.

00:06:26.594 --> 00:06:28.179
One night, the
boy woke suddenly,

00:06:28.262 --> 00:06:31.641
claiming he heard his
aunt's voice calling to him.

00:06:31.724 --> 00:06:33.392
Soon rumors spread of a figure

00:06:33.476 --> 00:06:35.770
sitting in a tree
at a nearby pond.

00:06:35.853 --> 00:06:37.647
Then after a heavy monsoon rain,

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:39.524
a midnight fog
encased the village.

00:06:39.607 --> 00:06:42.819
The boy again hears his
aunt calling to him,

00:06:42.902 --> 00:06:45.029
begging him to come
catch her fish.

00:06:45.113 --> 00:06:46.572
So he sneaks out of the house

00:06:46.656 --> 00:06:48.324
and begins to collect the fish,

00:06:48.407 --> 00:06:50.868
but then he spots a
figure in the trees.

00:06:50.952 --> 00:06:52.995
Fear brings the boy
back to his senses,

00:06:53.079 --> 00:06:55.331
and with much effort
he is able to run.

00:06:55.414 --> 00:06:57.708
But he doesn't get
far before a long arm

00:06:57.792 --> 00:06:59.669
snatches his basket of fish,

00:06:59.752 --> 00:07:02.004
and the boy falls
into a dead faint.

00:07:02.088 --> 00:07:05.466
Three days pass before
he regains consciousness.

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:08.511
Everyone warns his mother to
keep a close eye on the boy,

00:07:08.594 --> 00:07:12.056
for next time, he might have
his neck broken by the Nishi.

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:13.975
But the monster is relentless

00:07:14.058 --> 00:07:16.227
and lures the boy into the pond.

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:19.272
He walks straight in,
sinking below the surface.

00:07:19.355 --> 00:07:21.941
His body is dragged up
in the fisherman's nets

00:07:22.024 --> 00:07:23.943
the next morning.

00:07:24.026 --> 00:07:25.027
Elements in this
story give insight

00:07:25.111 --> 00:07:28.573
into some cultural
practices and taboos.

00:07:28.656 --> 00:07:30.616
It isn't farfetched that
the boy's affliction

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:34.370
was treated with a variety of
methods, including exorcism.

00:07:34.453 --> 00:07:36.873
Belief in spirit possession
and malicious sorcery

00:07:36.956 --> 00:07:41.085
is common in this region even
today, although controversial.

00:07:41.169 --> 00:07:43.546
Some religious sites
are known for exorcisms

00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:45.464
but face increased
scrutiny in the modern day.

00:07:45.548 --> 00:07:48.342
An afflicted person
might make a pilgrimage

00:07:48.426 --> 00:07:49.969
to one of these
temples or shrines

00:07:50.052 --> 00:07:52.471
to remove any negative spiritual
or supernatural ailment.

00:07:54.473 --> 00:07:56.184
Policy reforms and
growing awareness

00:07:56.267 --> 00:07:57.518
of mental health challenges

00:07:57.602 --> 00:08:00.354
have made these temples
and shrines divisive,

00:08:00.438 --> 00:08:02.106
arguing that they
encourage superstition

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:03.482
and can do more harm.

00:08:03.566 --> 00:08:05.318
But others counter
that they offer a form

00:08:05.401 --> 00:08:06.444
of traditional
alternative healing

00:08:06.527 --> 00:08:10.239
that should not be
dismissed or demonized.

00:08:10.323 --> 00:08:12.325
Like many other
night spirit stories,

00:08:12.408 --> 00:08:15.077
the victim wanders
off and dies at night.

00:08:15.161 --> 00:08:16.370
Because of this,

00:08:16.454 --> 00:08:18.539
Nishi influence is often
said to be responsible

00:08:18.623 --> 00:08:22.418
for a particular trance-like
state, sleepwalking.

00:08:22.501 --> 00:08:25.129
Fun fact, I used to
sleepwalk as a kid.

00:08:25.213 --> 00:08:27.757
One night, my parents found
me knee deep in our pool,

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:30.635
something I have zero memory of.

00:08:30.718 --> 00:08:33.846
Death by sleepwalking is rare,
but injuries aren't uncommon.

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:36.349
Perhaps the Nishi
serves as an explanation

00:08:36.432 --> 00:08:37.850
for this strange phenomenon

00:08:37.934 --> 00:08:39.435
and helps the
community stay alert

00:08:39.519 --> 00:08:41.229
and keep sleepwalkers safe.

00:08:41.312 --> 00:08:43.231
Nishi Daak stories are also told

00:08:43.314 --> 00:08:45.399
to make sense of
horrible illnesses.

00:08:45.483 --> 00:08:47.860
So let's say there's a child

00:08:47.944 --> 00:08:51.280
who, for no explainable reason,

00:08:51.364 --> 00:08:53.616
without any treatment,

00:08:53.699 --> 00:08:57.745
is getting sicker, kind of
wasting away in a family.

00:08:57.828 --> 00:09:00.248
You might say that the Nishi

00:09:00.331 --> 00:09:03.334
has called away
their life force.

00:09:03.417 --> 00:09:07.421
So the notion is that maybe
somewhere else in the village

00:09:07.505 --> 00:09:10.091
or somewhere in the locality,

00:09:10.174 --> 00:09:14.470
a wealthy family with an
ill child of their own

00:09:14.553 --> 00:09:19.392
has employed a tantric or
a magician of some sort

00:09:19.475 --> 00:09:21.727
to wander around
the neighborhood

00:09:21.811 --> 00:09:24.855
saying a particular
magical mantra

00:09:24.939 --> 00:09:29.735
and carrying around a
hollowed-out green coconut

00:09:29.819 --> 00:09:34.532
in which they hope to trap
the essence, the soul,

00:09:34.615 --> 00:09:38.202
or the life force of
a healthy young person

00:09:38.286 --> 00:09:42.373
and give it to this ill one.

00:09:42.456 --> 00:09:46.502
So in a community with high
rates of infant mortality

00:09:46.586 --> 00:09:48.713
and childhood illness,

00:09:48.796 --> 00:09:51.882
stories like those
of the Nishi Daak

00:09:51.966 --> 00:09:55.678
may have been a way for
families to make sense

00:09:55.761 --> 00:09:59.223
of what was completely
un-understandable

00:09:59.307 --> 00:10:01.642
and happening before their eyes.

00:10:01.726 --> 00:10:03.811
[Zarka] Let's go back to the
creepiest part of this monster

00:10:03.894 --> 00:10:05.896
and the thing that
literally defines it,

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:07.898
the disembodied
voice in the night.

00:10:09.442 --> 00:10:11.068
People have been trying to
understand the phenomena

00:10:11.152 --> 00:10:13.487
of the disembodied voice
for quite some time.

00:10:13.571 --> 00:10:16.282
From exploring the validity
of religious communications

00:10:16.365 --> 00:10:17.700
to assessing clairvoyance,

00:10:17.783 --> 00:10:19.410
even now, we look for answers

00:10:19.493 --> 00:10:21.829
in both the secular
and the scientific.

00:10:21.912 --> 00:10:23.748
As technology and urbanization
continue to evolve

00:10:23.831 --> 00:10:26.751
and literally bring
light to the darkness,

00:10:26.834 --> 00:10:28.544
so do the stories of the Nishi,

00:10:28.627 --> 00:10:31.297
but that doesn't make
them any less important.

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:32.548
As a Bengali immigrant
daughter to the US,

00:10:32.631 --> 00:10:38.262
Bengali folk stories,
stories about flying horses,

00:10:38.346 --> 00:10:42.767
princes and princesses,
evil serpent kings,

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:47.480
and even monsters like the
carnivorous, drooling rakkhosh

00:10:47.563 --> 00:10:51.484
and of course the
ever-present Bengali bhoot

00:10:51.567 --> 00:10:55.571
were literally what connected
me to my cultural roots.

00:10:55.654 --> 00:10:57.490
These were stories
that were told to me

00:10:57.573 --> 00:11:00.576
by not only my parents
but my grandparents.

00:11:00.659 --> 00:11:02.578
Oral stories are critical

00:11:02.661 --> 00:11:04.580
to communities
throughout the world.

00:11:04.663 --> 00:11:08.584
And I think particularly
to diasporic communities

00:11:08.668 --> 00:11:11.796
who are raising children
far from their homeland,

00:11:11.879 --> 00:11:15.966
folk stories, monster
stories, ghost stories

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:20.012
are a way to connect young
people to their origins,

00:11:20.096 --> 00:11:21.680
to their ancestors,

00:11:21.764 --> 00:11:25.393
and to their sources
of cultural belief.

00:11:25.476 --> 00:11:29.063
These stories are deliciously
frightening sometimes

00:11:29.146 --> 00:11:29.939
to listen to,

00:11:30.022 --> 00:11:32.608
but they're also
deeply meaningful.

00:11:32.692 --> 00:11:33.609
Somewhat ironically,

00:11:33.692 --> 00:11:35.611
given the Nishi
Daak's dangerous call,

00:11:35.694 --> 00:11:37.613
in speaking and
hearing their stories,

00:11:37.697 --> 00:11:40.199
we preserve their importance.

00:11:40.282 --> 00:11:41.992
The deceased corporeal...

00:11:42.076 --> 00:11:44.995
Community stay alert and
keep safewalkers safe.

00:11:45.079 --> 00:11:48.124
Oh my God, I started it
and couldn't stop myself.

00:11:48.207 --> 00:11:50.000
Nishi Daaks, Daaks.

